Here is the standard Java "Hello World" code snippet along with a brief explanation of its components.

### Java Hello World Snippet
```java
public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Prints "Hello, World!" to the terminal window.
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");
    }
}
```

### Code Structure Explanation
1.  **`public class HelloWorld`**: In Java, every line of code that can be executed must be inside a `class`. This line defines a class named `HelloWorld`. The filename must match the class name (e.g., `HelloWorld.java`).
2.  **`public static void main(String[] args)`**: This is the **main method**. It is the entry point for any Java application. When you run the program, the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) looks for this specific method to begin execution.
3.  **`System.out.println("Hello, World!");`**: This is a statement used to print text to the console. 
    *   `System` is a built-in Java class.
    *   `out` is a member of the System class that represents the standard output stream.
    *   `println` (print line) is the method that actually sends the string to the console and adds a new line.
